From afca3acb213021becab385eebe346269ac62ae8a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dominggoes Isakh <drjisakh@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 15:39:40 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 12/14] LoopbackEventHandler: don't abort when BPF ring buffers
 are unavailable

Same bug class as the LocalNetEventHandler fix (1a37eb72a8), different
ring buffer. system_server aborted during ConnectivityService init on
this device:

  E LoopbackEventHandler: BpfRingbuf init failed: failed to retrieve
    ringbuffer at /sys/fs/bpf/netd_shared/map_netd_loopback_access_ringbuf:
    Function not implemented
  F libc: Fatal signal 6 (SIGABRT) ... system_server

GetPoller() built its BpfRingbuf<LoopbackAccessEvent> with the single-arg
BpfRingbuf(path) constructor, which is documented in BpfRingbuf.h as
"Full construction that aborts on error (use Create/Init to handle
errors)". This device's 3.10 kernel has no BPF ring buffer support at
all (no CONFIG_BPF_SYSCALL equivalent), so Init() always fails here,
and the constructor's abort() took down system_server every boot.

Switch to the non-aborting BpfRingbuf<Value>::Create(path) factory
(already used correctly elsewhere, e.g. BpfRingbufSized::Create), which
returns a Result instead of aborting. On failure, log a warning and
return nullptr from GetPoller(), same as the pre-existing
RingbufEventPoller::Create() failure path right below it. Start() and
Stop() already null-check GetPoller()'s return value, and the single
caller (BpfEventPoller.cpp's init, via LoopbackEventHandler::Start())
already ignores the bool return, so loopback access usage reporting is
simply unavailable on this device instead of crash-looping the whole
system server.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Change-Id: Ife03bab631128dbca62dd3c178aa316643c646c5
---
 .../libs/libeventpolling/LoopbackEventHandler.cpp | 15 +++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/service/native/libs/libeventpolling/LoopbackEventHandler.cpp b/service/native/libs/libeventpolling/LoopbackEventHandler.cpp
index 942bbf653b..eb3a98d4c1 100644
--- a/service/native/libs/libeventpolling/LoopbackEventHandler.cpp
+++ b/service/native/libs/libeventpolling/LoopbackEventHandler.cpp
@@ -53,8 +53,19 @@ bpf::RingbufEventPoller<LoopbackAccessEvent> *
 LoopbackEventHandler::GetPoller() {
     static bpf::RingbufEventPoller<LoopbackAccessEvent> *const sPoller =
         []() -> bpf::RingbufEventPoller<LoopbackAccessEvent> * {
-        auto rb = std::make_unique<BpfRingbuf<LoopbackAccessEvent>>(
+        // Use the non-aborting Create() factory: devices without BPF ring
+        // buffer support in the kernel (e.g. no CONFIG_BPF_SYSCALL at all)
+        // can't ever create this, and that's not a fatal condition for the
+        // rest of the system - loopback access usage reporting is simply
+        // unavailable. The single-arg BpfRingbuf(path) constructor this
+        // intentionally avoids aborts on the same failure (see BpfRingbuf.h).
+        auto rbResult = BpfRingbuf<LoopbackAccessEvent>::Create(
             LOOPBACK_ACCESS_RINGBUF_NETD_PATH);
+        if (!rbResult.ok()) {
+            ALOGW("Failed to create loopback access ring buffer: %s",
+                  rbResult.error().message().c_str());
+            return nullptr;
+        }
 
         auto callback = [](const std::vector<LoopbackAccessEvent> &events) {
             for (const LoopbackAccessEvent &event : events) {
@@ -66,7 +77,7 @@ LoopbackEventHandler::GetPoller() {
         };
 
         auto result = RingbufEventPoller<LoopbackAccessEvent>::Create(
-            std::move(callback), std::move(rb));
+            std::move(callback), std::move(rbResult.value()));
 
         if (!result.ok()) {
             ALOGE("Failed to create RingbufEventPoller: %s",
